<h1>Round Up</h1>
<h2>August 28, 2026</h2>
Today, I look at Lindsay Clancy, Cofnas, a recent article by
 Kasparov, a webcam “pimp” recently arrested, as well as touching on
 Lua.
<a name="2026-08-28-lindsay-clancy"> </a>
<h3>Lindsay Clancy</h3>
<p>There are a <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-26-NYT.html">lot of  women</a>  
supporting Lindsay Clacy, a woman who systematically killed her three
 children. Even the <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-26-MotherJones.html">liberal press</a> 
is saying she shouldn’t be prosecuted for murder.
<p>While there is nuance in Clancy’s story, at the end of the day this
 comes off as an extreme case of a certain kind of woman. This kind of
 woman, when told that another woman has done something bad, will,
 without even knowing the woman, immediately justify the offending
 behavior, no matter how egregious. These kinds of women who defend
 another woman’s behavior, no matter what, are the kinds of women I put
 up strong, healthy boundaries with.
<a name="2026-08-28-cofnas-redux"> </a>
<h3>Cofnas redux</h3>
<p>While some academics feel <a href="blog:2026-08-26">Cofnas</a> 
should
 not have been placed on suspension, many more feel his <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-26-antiCofnas.html">“Race Realism” is downright racist</a> 
and has no place in academic
 discourse.
<p>I personally think it’s OK for Cofnas to have his views on race, even
 though I strongly disagree with him. Where he crossed the line was
 looking up a Black person’s academic papers to hound him and accuse him
 of plagiarism—that comes of to me as targeted harassment, even if the
 papers were in fact plagiarized.
<a name="2026-08-28-kasparov-on-woke-1"> </a>
<h3>Kasparov on “Woke 1”</h3>
<p>World Chess Champion <a href="Kasparov.html">Kasparov</a> 
is <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-27-Kasparov.html">no fan of  “Woke 1”</a>, 
and with good reason: The <a href="blog:2020-06-16">cancel culture</a> 
of that era suppressed free speech and reasonable
 discourse.
<a name="2026-08-28-webcam-pimp-arrested"> </a>
<h3>Webcam “pimp” arrested</h3>
<p>Nikita Tyukalo was a webcam “pimp”—he coerced and manipulated girls
 in to becoming pornographic webcam “models”. He <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-28-webcam.html">was arrested and currently is in jail</a>; 
the linked article describes
 his alleged misogyny, how he ran the operation, and what ultimately
 tipped off police to arrest him.
<p>While he was being very manipulative with those women, the women in
 question chose to be in an intimate relationship with someone who, as
 per the linked article, had a very deep hatred of women.
<a name="2026-08-28-why-i-stick-with-lua-5.1"> </a>
<h3>Why I stick with Lua 5.1</h3>
<p>Sometimes, people argue that Lua 5.1 should no longer be supported,
 and everyone should move on to Lua 5.5. This is not feasible.
<p>As <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-28-Lua.html">a poster on Lobsters</a>  
points out, PUC Lua breaks compatibility between point releases, and
 applications that embed Lua need to not break scripts used by the
 applications.
<p>There’s a reason I forked Lua 5.1 and named it <a href="https://samboy.github.io/MaraDNS/webpage/lunacy/">Lunacy</a>—that 
guarantees that any future changes to Lua don’t break
 the scripts I use to make this blog nor the scripts that make MaraDNS’
 documentation.


